.TH "libnvmf_tid_parse" 9 "libnvmf_tid_parse" "October 2026" "libnvme API manual" LINUX
.SH NAME
libnvmf_tid_parse \- Allocate a TID from a semicolon-separated key=value string.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "int" libnvmf_tid_parse
.BI "(struct libnvme_global_ctx *ctx "  ","
.BI "const char *str "  ","
.BI "struct libnvmf_tid **out "  ");"
.SH ARGUMENTS
.IP "ctx" 12
Global context used only for logging the diagnostics below.
.IP "str" 12
Input string, e.g. "transport=tcp;traddr=1.2.3.4;trsvcid=8009".
.IP "out" 12
The allocated TID on success; NULL on error.
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Recognized keys are the "nvme connect" option names: transport, traddr,
trsvcid, nqn, host-traddr, host-iface, hostnqn, hostid.  (The "nqn",
"host-traddr", and "host-iface" string keys map onto the struct's
subsysnqn, host_traddr, and host_iface fields, whose names follow the
C-identifier convention.)  Unknown keys, bare keys (no '='), and empty
values are logged at WARN level and skipped.
Whitespace around keys and values is trimmed.
Like \fBlibnvmf_tid_from_fields\fP, a traddr/host-traddr that is not numeric on
an IP transport fails the whole parse.
.SH "RETURN"
0 on success (*\fIout\fP is the allocated TID); -EINVAL if \fIctx\fP, \fIout\fP or
\fIstr\fP is NULL, or a traddr/host-traddr is not numeric on an IP transport;
-ENOMEM on allocation failure.
